{#
  R1: the lab end of the IPsec tunnel to the AWS VM-Series.

  Route-based (VTI), matching the firewall's tunnel interface - a policy-based
  tunnel cannot pair with PAN-OS's route-based one, and that mismatch presents
  as phase 2 never completing with both ends insisting their proposals are fine.

  Why a template and not resource modules: cisco.ios models no crypto at all -
  no ikev2 proposal, policy, keyring or profile, no transform set, no ipsec
  profile, and no tunnel-interface protection. This is the same call as
  router_on_a_stick.j2, for a stronger reason.

  READ BEFORE COMMITTING. Two lines here can cut the lab off:

    - `ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 Tunnel1` moves ALL lab egress through AWS. It is
      guarded by vpn_default_via_tunnel, which defaults to FALSE. Bring the
      tunnel up first, prove traffic crosses it, and only then re-run with
      -e vpn_default_via_tunnel=true. Pushing it with the tunnel down replaces a
      working default route with a black hole.
    - the tunnel source is Gi0/1, the transit interface to the local firewall.
      That is also how R1 reaches the internet today, so it must keep its own
      reachable default route while the tunnel is being built.

  Everything renders from inventory (group_vars/palo_alto_aws + its vault, read
  through hostvars['PA-AWS']), so the addressing and the PSK are not in this
  file. Both ends read the same crypto variables, which is what stops them
  disagreeing about a proposal.

  ONE THING TO KNOW ABOUT THE RENDERED OUTPUT: it contains the pre-shared key in
  cleartext, because that is what the device command takes. It lands in
  ansible/reports/diffs/R1_candidate.cfg, mode 0640, in a directory gitignored at
  the repository root - but it is a secret sitting on the controller's disk, and
  it stays there until something removes it. Treat that directory the way
  backups/ and captures/ are treated elsewhere in this repo.
#}
{% set fw = hostvars['PA-AWS'] %}
{% set ike = fw.panos_vpn_ike_crypto %}
{% set esp = fw.panos_vpn_ipsec_crypto %}
crypto ikev2 proposal AWS-PROPOSAL
 encryption aes-cbc-256
 integrity sha256
 group 14
!
crypto ikev2 policy AWS-POLICY
 proposal AWS-PROPOSAL
!
{# The peer is matched by ADDRESS here, unlike the firewall's end which matches
   an identity: the firewall has a static Elastic IP, so this end knows exactly
   who it is calling. The asymmetry is correct - only one end of a
   dynamic-to-static pair can match on address. #}
crypto ikev2 keyring AWS-KEYRING
 peer PA-AWS
  address {{ fw.ansible_host }}
  pre-shared-key {{ fw.panos_vpn_psk }}
 !
!
crypto ikev2 profile AWS-PROFILE
 match identity remote address {{ fw.ansible_host }} 255.255.255.255
{# The identity this end SENDS. It is what the firewall matches on, because this
   end's address is dynamic - so this string and the firewall's peer_id_value
   are the same variable, and a tunnel that authenticates but never establishes
   is usually these two having drifted apart. #}
 identity local fqdn {{ fw.panos_vpn_peer_id }}
 authentication local pre-share
 authentication remote pre-share
 keyring local AWS-KEYRING
{# Dead peer detection, and not optional on a tunnel across a home connection:
   without it this end keeps an SA the far side has long forgotten, and traffic
   black-holes until something rekeys. #}
 dpd 10 3 periodic
!
crypto ipsec transform-set AWS-TS esp-aes 256 esp-sha256-hmac
 mode tunnel
!
crypto ipsec profile AWS-IPSEC
 set transform-set AWS-TS
 set ikev2-profile AWS-PROFILE
!
interface Tunnel1
 description IPsec to PA-AWS ({{ esp.name }}/{{ ike.name }})
 ip address {{ fw.panos_vpn_peer_tunnel_ip }} 255.255.255.252
 tunnel source {{ transit_interface }}
 tunnel mode ipsec ipv4
 tunnel destination {{ fw.ansible_host }}
 tunnel protection ipsec profile AWS-IPSEC
{# 1379 = 1500 - 20 IP - 20 TCP - ESP/IKE overhead on a NAT-T tunnel. Without a
   clamp, large packets need fragmentation that home connections frequently drop,
   and the symptom is the worst kind: ping works, SSH connects, and anything that
   moves real data hangs. #}
 ip tcp adjust-mss 1379
 no shutdown
!
{% for net in vpn_remote_networks | default([]) %}
ip route {{ net.network }} {{ net.netmask }} Tunnel1
{% endfor %}
{% if vpn_default_via_tunnel | default(false) %}
{# Guarded, and the guard is the point - see this file's header. #}
ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 Tunnel1
{% endif %}
